WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:06.550
Welcome to Ear Biscuits, I'm Rhett.

00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:07.750
And I'm Link.

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:10.940
This week at the round
table of dim lighting,

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:14.710
it's still not that dim and
we're still not at the table

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:17.310
because we're still at our own homes.

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:18.191
How do you think the table is doing?

00:00:18.191 --> 00:00:19.910
How long is this gonna last?

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:22.150
The table's just sitting
in there in the studio,

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:22.983
just being a table.

00:00:22.983 --> 00:00:24.710
Oh, I'm sure it's doing fine.

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:26.430
Yeah, it's doing good, man.

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:30.130
It's lonely, sadly, like a
lot of people these days.

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:31.640
Don't mean to start on the downer.

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:34.860
We are gonna talk about the silver lining.

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:36.390
We're gonna try to be positive,

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:39.880
and we're gonna look to the future.

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:44.863
And what type of impact is
this experience going through,

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:48.273
weathering this virus,

00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:51.710
what lasting effects is it gonna have?

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:53.530
What ripple effects is it gonna have?

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:58.520
How is it gonna impact the
future of our lives indefinitely?

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:01.240
And like you said, I
think in a positive way,

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:02.250
'cause I mean, one of the things

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:07.250
that has been helpful for me
as I get anxious, get fearful,

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:15.640
get frustrated with the fact that we're

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:19.840
cooped up in the house is to think

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:21.440
about the good that will come from this.

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:23.890
And I do, I think there's gonna be,

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:28.420
there's quite a bit, there's
quite a bit to be excited

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:29.893
about in terms of the future.

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:31.903
It's growing pains.
Yeah, I've got

00:01:31.903 --> 00:01:32.736
a number of thoughts

00:01:32.736 --> 00:01:35.349
related to different categories.

00:01:35.349 --> 00:01:39.180
As I'm adapting to this new lifestyle

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:42.143
of staying at home with
my immediate family,

00:01:44.148 --> 00:01:45.510
I'm starting to take note of things.

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:46.860
It's like, wow, this is,

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:49.570
this is different, but it's not worse.

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:51.030
This is better.

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:53.340
I wonder if I'm gonna keep this habit

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:54.660
even after things change.

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:57.130
So, you know, I've got a number of things

00:01:57.130 --> 00:01:58.753
that I'll bring up.

00:02:00.073 --> 00:02:01.070
And then, you know what?

00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:04.120
This can serve as kind of
like a time capsule episode

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:04.953
where we go back to 'em

00:02:04.953 --> 00:02:07.020
and we see how right or wrong we were.

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:08.970
Do you have any bold predictions

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:10.420
that you're prepared to make?

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:17.630
Well, my prediction is that at some point

00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:19.930
I won't be in the corner of my living room

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:23.690
making this podcast, as is
evidenced just by the fact

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:26.000
that right at the beginning
of this recording,

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:28.880
when I got, which I had to
do every time we get ready

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:31.127
to do anything in here, is I'm like,

00:02:31.127 --> 00:02:32.620
"Okay, guys, I'm recording."

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:35.100
And then the thing that came back,

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:36.530
one person said,
Noice.

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:39.520
I think my wife said,
"Can't we live our lives?"

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:41.557
And then I think my son said,

00:02:41.557 --> 00:02:43.210
"Why don't you do this in the garage?"

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:46.870
So again, the family has still not adapted

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:49.550
to what's happening, even though,

00:02:49.550 --> 00:02:51.119
I mean, I hate to drive this point home--

00:02:51.119 --> 00:02:52.380
And you're not talking
about the virus anymore.

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:55.100
You're talking about your job,

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:57.670
being in their faces.
Interestingly, you know,

00:02:57.670 --> 00:03:01.460
my job is actually an important part

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:03.830
of the family dynamic.

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:04.993
Last time I checked.

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:10.680
Now, my wonderful,
beautiful wife is standing

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:14.970
at the top of the steps down
into our sunken living room

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:17.610
and just staring at me incredulously

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:18.650
because she's listening.

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:19.483
She looks great.

00:03:19.483 --> 00:03:21.233
She's got her Jason Isbell shirt on

00:03:21.233 --> 00:03:23.003
that has a dinosaur on it.

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:26.610
Why are you reducing her to
only what she looks like?

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:29.570
Well, I'm not doing that.

00:03:29.570 --> 00:03:31.740
I just know, I know what she likes.

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:34.173
I know how she likes me to talk about her.

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:37.063
I love you.

00:03:37.063 --> 00:03:37.896
I love all of you.

00:03:37.896 --> 00:03:38.763
I love all of you.

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:41.640
You're all great.
Now, go away.

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:47.440
Well, one of the things that I have been,

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:49.240
that has been an immediate change for me

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:50.740
is the way that we're approaching food.

00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:54.970
I think this is the case for
people all over the world.

00:03:54.970 --> 00:03:58.450
Now, we are in a very privileged position

00:03:58.450 --> 00:04:01.210
because we still have access

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:05.800
to all the food that we
want essentially, right?

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:07.830
You may not get as many things

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:10.840
as readily as you got them in the past

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:12.960
and we're making do
with a little bit more.

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:16.820
We're cooking for ourselves a
lot more than we normally do.

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:21.070
We're eating leftovers which,

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:23.940
this is gonna sound pretty privileged,

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:27.950
but most of the leftovers at our house

00:04:27.950 --> 00:04:30.680
get eaten by the adults.

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:31.913
I'll eat leftovers.

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:34.853
My kids are spoiled rotten, man.

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:37.093
I'll eat leftovers.

00:04:37.986 --> 00:04:40.700
If another day comes, the next day comes

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:42.520
and we're like, "We're having leftovers,"

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:46.880
the look of grief and disappointment
on my children's faces

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:49.473
is just a sign that
they're spoiled rotten.

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:52.777
But now--
We would have a night--

00:04:52.777 --> 00:04:54.000
We don't have a choice at this point.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:55.770
We would have a night of the week

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:57.930
that would be leftover night,
and we tried to make it

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:00.430
into something that
seemed more like an event.

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:05.220
Like Christy would say things
like, "It's leftover night,"

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:07.420
you know, and you're just like--

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:09.630
How did that go over?
Am I supposed to cheer?

00:05:09.630 --> 00:05:11.410
It's not usually cheers.

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:12.920
No.

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:15.343
But yeah, now there's
a heightened sense of,

00:05:16.510 --> 00:05:18.420
okay, there's a little bit of this left.

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:22.300
I'ma Tupperware that thing
up and I'ma put it away.

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:25.010
And then you're, 'cause
you just don't know

00:05:25.010 --> 00:05:26.853
when you're gonna get some of that

00:05:26.853 --> 00:05:29.050
from the grocery store again.

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:31.430
Like a little bit of, we had,

00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:34.200
I mean, we are occasionally
ordering takeout.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:36.570
It's a stressful time.

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:39.570
Especially after that video
that Ellie sent around.

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:41.080
Ellie shared a video,

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:43.980
and there's this one doctor,
I guess he's in Seattle, who,

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:47.360
he's had a few videos go viral,

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:50.260
but he talks about what you should do

00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:51.460
when you bring groceries home

00:05:51.460 --> 00:05:53.220
and when you bring takeout home.

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:55.240
And I shared that video with

00:05:56.570 --> 00:06:00.580
our friend chat group, which
Jessie, of course, is in.

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:03.097
And she was like, "You
cannot share this video

00:06:03.097 --> 00:06:04.647
"with someone who has OCD,

00:06:04.647 --> 00:06:07.330
"because they will just
go down this rabbit hole."

00:06:07.330 --> 00:06:09.380
So I said, okay--
And never come back.

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:11.520
I'll be the one to be responsible

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:14.600
for the disinfection process
when things come to the door.

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:16.633
It's very stressful.

00:06:17.630 --> 00:06:19.600
It's one thing when
you've got like a couple,

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:22.690
like two bags of carryout,
but it's another thing

00:06:22.690 --> 00:06:25.960
when you order groceries and they show up.

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:29.230
Like yesterday, we had groceries
show up, which, by the way,

00:06:29.230 --> 00:06:34.230
we had ordered probably
five or six days in advance.

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:37.010
And so you're trying to figure out

00:06:37.010 --> 00:06:39.570
what you're gonna be out
of, and you're gonna,

00:06:39.570 --> 00:06:44.420
you're trying to figure out,
well, if they don't have this,

00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:45.610
then they're gonna try to substitute.

00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:46.443
If they don't have anything,

00:06:46.443 --> 00:06:48.660
I'm trying to get something
else just so we got,

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:50.160
we have well-rounded meals.

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:52.420
Now Christy handled that order,

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:54.940
but I knew how stressful it would be

00:06:54.940 --> 00:06:56.960
when all that was dropped off at the door.

00:06:56.960 --> 00:06:59.590
I was like, I'll be on the front lines

00:06:59.590 --> 00:07:02.070
of kinda bringing this stuff in.

00:07:02.070 --> 00:07:04.220
And you know, we got this whole system.

00:07:04.220 --> 00:07:06.067
Like, we clean off the counter
and then you bring it in

00:07:06.067 --> 00:07:07.730
and you put all the bags on the floor.

00:07:07.730 --> 00:07:09.060
and you take the stuff out of the bags

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:13.210
and you put 'em in a unsanitized pile,

00:07:13.210 --> 00:07:18.210
throw away the bags, and then
spray down and then wipe down

00:07:20.310 --> 00:07:24.187
every square inch of
everything that's pre-packaged

00:07:24.187 --> 00:07:26.860
that's potentially been handled

00:07:26.860 --> 00:07:28.560
that's coming into the house.

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:33.290
I mean, it's a, you know,
it was a lot of groceries,

00:07:33.290 --> 00:07:34.610
so it was for five people,

00:07:34.610 --> 00:07:38.630
so it was like a half-hour
process to get this thing done.

00:07:38.630 --> 00:07:42.163
And it's very stressful
because you start to feel like,

00:07:43.550 --> 00:07:47.470
you start to feel like you're exiting

00:07:47.470 --> 00:07:52.323
some sort of nuclear contamination zone.

00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:57.940
To me, that's one of the first things

00:07:57.940 --> 00:07:58.930
I've been thinking about,

00:07:58.930 --> 00:08:03.930
is as much as I overthink everything,

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:08.743
now I've been thinking about
the level of contagion,

00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:11.650
how easily things can be spread

00:08:11.650 --> 00:08:14.000
and all the things that I'm touching.

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:16.700
So I mean, this is, I'm sure,

00:08:16.700 --> 00:08:19.020
a common experience to a lot of people,

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:21.990
and I just don't know
if that's gonna go away

00:08:21.990 --> 00:08:26.450
when this virus is in the past, you know.

00:08:26.450 --> 00:08:28.020
Am I gonna be a fist bumper?

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:29.950
Am I gonna be a, am I gonna be somebody

00:08:29.950 --> 00:08:33.430
who brings in the groceries
and wipes them down?

00:08:33.430 --> 00:08:35.190
I don't think I'll be
wiping all the groceries.

00:08:35.190 --> 00:08:37.890
No, because that would be irrational.

00:08:37.890 --> 00:08:39.040
I mean--
Oh, I know,

00:08:40.330 --> 00:08:43.720
but I will be thinking
about it more often.

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:46.590
I mean, like not handshaking
or touching people

00:08:46.590 --> 00:08:48.010
for such a long time.
Well, yeah.

00:08:48.010 --> 00:08:48.843
Let's wait.

00:08:48.843 --> 00:08:50.430
Let's wait to get into
that because I think

00:08:50.430 --> 00:08:52.460
that there's a whole
conversation we can have about

00:08:52.460 --> 00:08:55.080
like what this means
about proximity to people

00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:56.210
and greetings and stuff.

00:08:56.210 --> 00:08:58.880
But going back to the groceries, I mean,

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:00.211
the first thing I will say,

00:09:00.211 --> 00:09:01.370
because I don't wanna alarm people,

00:09:01.370 --> 00:09:03.720
I don't want people to
think that the process

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:06.440
that you just outlined is
necessary for everybody

00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:11.440
because, you know, most of the research,

00:09:12.940 --> 00:09:17.940
the research suggests that the
virus does not easily spread

00:09:18.660 --> 00:09:20.800
through takeout food or through groceries.

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:22.610
But that doesn't mean
that there isn't a risk.

00:09:22.610 --> 00:09:24.670
It just means that it's
significantly lower a risk

00:09:24.670 --> 00:09:27.230
than actually being in
the proximity of somebody

00:09:27.230 --> 00:09:28.750
who is infected and breathing in

00:09:28.750 --> 00:09:31.549
like an actual, you know, droplet

00:09:31.549 --> 00:09:33.882
that's in the air.
Droplet.

00:09:35.270 --> 00:09:37.160
But that doesn't mean that
there isn't some risk.

00:09:37.160 --> 00:09:40.070
So if you wanna eliminate the
risk or minimize the risk,

00:09:40.070 --> 00:09:41.620
then you can go the extra mile.

00:09:41.620 --> 00:09:43.350
Not doing, you sound like you're doing

00:09:43.350 --> 00:09:46.340
exactly what the guy
described in the video.

00:09:46.340 --> 00:09:47.352
He got all the way down--

00:09:47.352 --> 00:09:48.580
Yeah, once you send me the video,

00:09:48.580 --> 00:09:50.590
I'm gonna do exactly what's in the video.

00:09:50.590 --> 00:09:52.740
Yeah, but he was washing
each piece of fruit

00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:54.830
for 20 seconds with soap.

00:09:54.830 --> 00:09:56.830
You're not doing that, are you?

00:09:56.830 --> 00:09:58.130
Christy handled that stuff,

00:09:58.130 --> 00:10:00.670
so I don't know if she went that far.

00:10:00.670 --> 00:10:01.981
We're just being--
Probably did though.

00:10:01.981 --> 00:10:05.220
We're trying to leave the external bags

00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:07.300
and we're doing takeout
like once to twice a week.

00:10:07.300 --> 00:10:09.260
We're trying to minimize that.

00:10:09.260 --> 00:10:11.600
But leaving those external
bags on the porch,

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:14.320
and then when you can kinda empty out

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:17.700
the contents of a package onto a plate

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:20.160
or something like that and
then throwing away that

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:21.730
and wiping everything down,

00:10:21.730 --> 00:10:23.630
sticking to hot foods
instead of cold foods,

00:10:23.630 --> 00:10:25.520
it, I just, what I'm trying to do

00:10:25.520 --> 00:10:28.920
is mitigate it, minimize it,

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:32.923
but knowing that you can't
really eliminate the risk.

00:10:33.770 --> 00:10:35.970
But I mean, the thing that
scares me more than anything

00:10:35.970 --> 00:10:40.160
is if I have to be in close
proximity with somebody,

00:10:40.160 --> 00:10:40.993
which I really haven't.

00:10:40.993 --> 00:10:43.117
The only, during this,

00:10:43.117 --> 00:10:45.033
and so we're recording this on,

00:10:46.682 --> 00:10:49.021
what is it, the 31st of March

00:10:49.021 --> 00:10:50.520
when we're actually recording this?

00:10:50.520 --> 00:10:51.353
Yeah.

00:10:51.353 --> 00:10:54.923
And so obviously, you're
still in lockdown.

00:10:54.923 --> 00:10:58.550
Lockdown's been, sort
of a federal lockdown

00:10:58.550 --> 00:11:01.510
has been pushed through the end of April.

00:11:01.510 --> 00:11:04.370
California will probably
go longer than that.

00:11:04.370 --> 00:11:08.870
I saw that the governor of Virginia

00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:12.450
said there was gonna be a
lockdown through June 10th,

00:11:12.450 --> 00:11:13.550
subject to be updated.

00:11:13.550 --> 00:11:15.380
But he went ahead and said to June 10th,

00:11:15.380 --> 00:11:17.730
a stay at home order, basically.

00:11:17.730 --> 00:11:21.330
The only thing that I've done
is there was that one time

00:11:21.330 --> 00:11:23.620
I had to go over to the creative house

00:11:23.620 --> 00:11:27.040
to accept the delivery of a desk.

00:11:27.040 --> 00:11:29.160
My desk finally came in.

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:30.440
And I met the guy--
How'd that go?

00:11:30.440 --> 00:11:35.440
Well, I met the guys out
in the front and I stayed,

00:11:36.310 --> 00:11:39.150
I was like 20 feet from
these dudes outside.

00:11:39.150 --> 00:11:41.067
And I was like, "All right,
guys, here's what I'm gonna do.

00:11:41.067 --> 00:11:43.112
"I'm going to describe to you,

00:11:43.112 --> 00:11:46.227
"I'm gonna go inside, I'm
gonna open up the doors

00:11:46.227 --> 00:11:48.327
"and leave them open so you can walk in.

00:11:48.327 --> 00:11:49.257
"I'm gonna come back out.

00:11:49.257 --> 00:11:52.417
"I'm gonna describe to you the room

00:11:52.417 --> 00:11:54.907
"and the location that I want
this desk to be set up in.

00:11:54.907 --> 00:11:57.397
"And then I'm gonna go into the backyard

00:11:57.397 --> 00:11:59.330
"and I'm going to wait."

00:11:59.330 --> 00:12:00.380
So in other words, I don't wanna be

00:12:00.380 --> 00:12:02.900
in the same house at the same time.

00:12:02.900 --> 00:12:05.883
Because I've been doing good
about this quarantine thing.

00:12:07.885 --> 00:12:09.560
And now, first of all, they,

00:12:09.560 --> 00:12:11.100
the two guys who were delivering

00:12:11.100 --> 00:12:12.840
and setting up the desk volunteered,

00:12:12.840 --> 00:12:15.940
they were like, "Well, we don't have it."

00:12:15.940 --> 00:12:17.740
And I was like, I didn't say anything,

00:12:17.740 --> 00:12:20.457
like, "Well, you're typically asymptom-,

00:12:20.457 --> 00:12:22.587
"you could be the 25% or more of people

00:12:22.587 --> 00:12:25.707
"who are asymptomatic, who have
it but are still contagious.

00:12:25.707 --> 00:12:28.177
"Or you could be the 100% of people

00:12:28.177 --> 00:12:30.417
"who usually get the disease and have it,

00:12:30.417 --> 00:12:31.987
"are asymptomatic for the first two days

00:12:31.987 --> 00:12:33.240
"but also are contagious."

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:34.990
But I didn't break that out.

00:12:34.990 --> 00:12:35.823
I didn't wanna be that guy.

00:12:35.823 --> 00:12:36.656
It's frustrating that it's just

00:12:36.656 --> 00:12:38.440
not getting through to people.

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:40.280
Well, that's another
thing we'll talk about,

00:12:40.280 --> 00:12:43.560
is people's inability or unwillingness

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:46.810
to process the facts around this stuff.

00:12:46.810 --> 00:12:50.540
But anyway, so I said all that,

00:12:50.540 --> 00:12:54.370
and then I'm in the backyard
and the guy, he's like, "Sir."

00:12:54.370 --> 00:12:57.690
And I'm like, "Oh, no,
they've run into a problem."

00:12:57.690 --> 00:13:00.550
And I kinda like stick my
head into the living room

00:13:00.550 --> 00:13:01.730
and I can see in the hallway,

00:13:01.730 --> 00:13:03.220
and I can see that they've got the desk

00:13:03.220 --> 00:13:05.947
sort of like stood up and they're like,

00:13:05.947 --> 00:13:09.167
"This desk is not going to fit into,

00:13:09.167 --> 00:13:10.527
"we can't get it in
there," and he was like,

00:13:10.527 --> 00:13:12.367
"I've been doing this for 13 years."

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:16.320
At that point,
It ain't gonna happen.

00:13:16.320 --> 00:13:18.340
I took a deep breath.

00:13:18.340 --> 00:13:21.420
And you told him to
just put it in my room.

00:13:21.420 --> 00:13:23.350
Well, actually, no, no--
It's a nice desk.

00:13:23.350 --> 00:13:25.840
No, I explored this option.

00:13:25.840 --> 00:13:26.673
I was gonna do this

00:13:26.673 --> 00:13:28.000
without your permission.
Oh, yeah?

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:30.457
I was gonna put my desk in your room

00:13:30.457 --> 00:13:33.860
and just switch our offices
and see if you ever noticed.

00:13:33.860 --> 00:13:36.580
But it's not the location of my office,

00:13:36.580 --> 00:13:40.240
it's the width of the
doors in the creative house

00:13:40.240 --> 00:13:45.240
are 29 inches, which are too
small to get anything in there.

00:13:45.340 --> 00:13:47.990
So you're, there's no
room that it could fit in

00:13:47.990 --> 00:13:50.170
except the living room, so
I told him to take it back.

00:13:50.170 --> 00:13:52.930
But before I told him to take
it back, I went up to them,

00:13:52.930 --> 00:13:54.980
I held my breath and
walked into the house.

00:13:54.980 --> 00:13:57.350
You know I can hold my
breath for quite some time.

00:13:57.350 --> 00:14:00.040
I had a conversation without breathing,

00:14:00.040 --> 00:14:02.070
which I have been practicing lately.

00:14:02.070 --> 00:14:03.770
Which we've discussed, yes.

00:14:03.770 --> 00:14:06.380
And I was like, okay,

00:14:06.380 --> 00:14:07.452
by the time I was running out of breath,

00:14:07.452 --> 00:14:08.389
I was like, "All right, you're right.

00:14:08.389 --> 00:14:09.437
"I don't think this is gonna fit.

00:14:09.437 --> 00:14:11.580
"Just return it," and then ran outside.

00:14:11.580 --> 00:14:12.480
You ran outside?

00:14:14.150 --> 00:14:15.296
Yeah.

00:14:15.296 --> 00:14:16.210
It's like your face is turning blue.

00:14:16.210 --> 00:14:19.130
I probably did breathe
inside a little bit so,

00:14:19.130 --> 00:14:21.110
but you know, that was a week ago

00:14:21.110 --> 00:14:23.953
and I'm still feeling okay.

00:14:25.041 --> 00:14:25.990
Anyway--
I mean,

00:14:25.990 --> 00:14:27.820
I get as much delivered as possible,

00:14:27.820 --> 00:14:31.930
but I ordered takeout from this one place

00:14:31.930 --> 00:14:34.500
and I went to pick it up.

00:14:34.500 --> 00:14:35.860
And I had done this before

00:14:35.860 --> 00:14:37.550
and you know, you go in
and they've got a rack

00:14:37.550 --> 00:14:38.770
and your stuff's on it.

00:14:38.770 --> 00:14:39.700
We've already paid.

00:14:39.700 --> 00:14:41.570
There's no touching the credit cards

00:14:41.570 --> 00:14:43.120
or even signing of anything.

00:14:43.120 --> 00:14:44.300
You just take it.

00:14:44.300 --> 00:14:45.545
But some people, they're not,

00:14:45.545 --> 00:14:47.360
they haven't figured all that out.

00:14:47.360 --> 00:14:51.600
So I get there and it's
like, I gotta sign a thing.

00:14:51.600 --> 00:14:53.280
I'm not, I didn't wanna sign a thing.

00:14:53.280 --> 00:14:55.550
And I'm touching this pen.

00:14:55.550 --> 00:14:56.383
Yeah, what the hell?

00:14:56.383 --> 00:14:58.560
Why are they, what is
wrong with people, man?

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:00.940
I don't understand why they
can't get with the program.

00:15:00.940 --> 00:15:02.370
Figure it out.

00:15:02.370 --> 00:15:06.860
And then, even worse,
they have this coffee bar,

00:15:06.860 --> 00:15:09.410
and that's where you went
to pick up the stuff,

00:15:09.410 --> 00:15:10.970
but there was a guy sitting--

00:15:10.970 --> 00:15:11.803
You should have just said,

00:15:11.803 --> 00:15:13.690
"I'm not signing it," by the way.

00:15:13.690 --> 00:15:14.542
Say, "Screw it.

00:15:14.542 --> 00:15:15.587
"What are you gonna do, sue me?

00:15:15.587 --> 00:15:17.320
"I'm not signing it."

00:15:17.320 --> 00:15:22.140
I signed it and I had
my antibacterial spray,

00:15:22.140 --> 00:15:24.710
and I immediately sprayed
myself and walked out

00:15:24.710 --> 00:15:28.890
and sprayed myself again,
sprayed my hand, you know, but--

00:15:28.890 --> 00:15:31.300
Sprayed myself.
There was a guy

00:15:31.300 --> 00:15:34.240
who had gotten coffee, and they gave it

00:15:34.240 --> 00:15:35.800
to him in a to-go mug,

00:15:35.800 --> 00:15:38.370
and then he sat down in the aisle

00:15:38.370 --> 00:15:39.840
where everyone has to pick up.

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:43.830
And he's like just within
a few feet of people,

00:15:43.830 --> 00:15:45.453
and he's an older guy.

00:15:46.860 --> 00:15:49.230
And he's talking to
everybody that comes in

00:15:49.230 --> 00:15:52.220
because, I don't know,
maybe he lives alone.

00:15:52.220 --> 00:15:53.710
He's probably lonely.

00:15:53.710 --> 00:15:54.990
He just sat there.

00:15:54.990 --> 00:15:57.920
And they should have
told him he had to leave.

00:15:57.920 --> 00:16:02.267
And here I am, I'm like, am
I the one to tell this guy,

00:16:02.267 --> 00:16:04.077
"Sir, you really don't need to be here

00:16:04.077 --> 00:16:06.587
"for your health and the
health of other people.

00:16:06.587 --> 00:16:09.827
"Everyone has to come in
too close proximity to you

00:16:09.827 --> 00:16:11.247
"and you're talking."

00:16:15.540 --> 00:16:19.270
What's the word when you
like, you boom your voice?

00:16:19.270 --> 00:16:20.640
Projecting.
You're projecting.

00:16:20.640 --> 00:16:22.810
You're projecting your voice
so that people can hear you

00:16:22.810 --> 00:16:27.140
at a distance, which is projecting
stuff into the air, man.

00:16:27.140 --> 00:16:28.120
It's like--

00:16:28.120 --> 00:16:30.010
Well, just to give you an idea--

00:16:30.010 --> 00:16:31.670
This is why I didn't talk to the guy,

00:16:31.670 --> 00:16:33.160
because if I started talking to him,

00:16:33.160 --> 00:16:34.910
I didn't wanna get angry with him.

00:16:34.910 --> 00:16:36.510
So I didn't say anything.

00:16:36.510 --> 00:16:39.267
Well, and so there might
be people who think that,

00:16:39.267 --> 00:16:41.290
and we're gonna talk about this.

00:16:41.290 --> 00:16:42.123
There might be people
Just talk about it.

00:16:42.123 --> 00:16:45.600
Who think that we're overreacting,

00:16:45.600 --> 00:16:48.587
that we're overreacting, that
the experts are overreacting.

00:16:48.587 --> 00:16:49.443
But just to give,

00:16:49.443 --> 00:16:53.200
what you're talking about with
the projecting of the voices.

00:16:53.200 --> 00:16:57.710
So did you hear about the
chorus group that got together?

00:16:57.710 --> 00:17:00.490
60 people got together--
No.

00:17:00.490 --> 00:17:02.430
Like, you know, a week or two ago.

00:17:02.430 --> 00:17:05.000
I don't know, before
everyone really understood,

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:06.230
maybe two, three weeks ago,

00:17:06.230 --> 00:17:07.510
before anyone really understood

00:17:07.510 --> 00:17:09.360
the seriousness of what was going on.

00:17:10.890 --> 00:17:14.280
They got together for one practice

00:17:14.280 --> 00:17:17.300
in a room, all singing, 60 people.

00:17:17.300 --> 00:17:20.453
45 of them have tested
positive for COVID-19.

00:17:22.510 --> 00:17:26.830
Being in the same room with
people who are talking,

00:17:26.830 --> 00:17:30.343
much less singing--
Projecting and singing.

00:17:31.870 --> 00:17:34.540
Just breathing is a risk.

00:17:34.540 --> 00:17:35.540
It's like, this is,

00:17:35.540 --> 00:17:39.070
again, this is not, this isn't,
it is not rocket science.

00:17:39.070 --> 00:17:42.110
It is science, but it isn't a mystery.

00:17:42.110 --> 00:17:43.390
It's not a surprise.

00:17:43.390 --> 00:17:44.223
Because people are like,

00:17:44.223 --> 00:17:45.056
"We didn't know this was gonna happen.

00:17:45.056 --> 00:17:47.250
"We don't, this is a surprise
how quickly it's spreading."

00:17:47.250 --> 00:17:49.563
No, it is not.

00:17:49.563 --> 00:17:52.120
People have been talking
about it the whole time.

00:17:52.120 --> 00:17:53.800
Why are we so surprised?

00:17:53.800 --> 00:17:55.980
So all right, let's take a short break.

00:17:55.980 --> 00:17:57.170
And it's scary.

00:17:57.170 --> 00:17:58.770
I just wanna get the
scary part out of the way

00:17:58.770 --> 00:18:01.000
so that on the backside of this break,

00:18:01.000 --> 00:18:03.943
we can be more positive,
because now I'm worked up.

00:18:04.780 --> 00:18:06.180
I know with some of the
stuff we're gonna talk about,

00:18:06.180 --> 00:18:07.390
we're gonna get worked up again.

00:18:07.390 --> 00:18:09.700
Oh, I'm gonna get worked up.

00:18:09.700 --> 00:18:11.600
You know I'm gonna get worked up.

00:18:11.600 --> 00:18:12.800
There's people dying.

00:18:12.800 --> 00:18:17.160
I mean, you know, Joe Diffie is dead, man.

00:18:17.160 --> 00:18:17.993
Right.

00:18:17.993 --> 00:18:19.460
Poor guy.

00:18:19.460 --> 00:18:20.683
You know, it's like,

00:18:21.560 --> 00:18:24.010
I have a lot of good
memories to Joe Diffie songs.

00:18:24.010 --> 00:18:27.000
I actually felt really
sad, you know, it's like--

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:30.100
But he does have, he has
the quintessential song

00:18:30.100 --> 00:18:32.240
about what to do to him
after he dies, though.

00:18:32.240 --> 00:18:35.293
Prop me up beside the jukebox when I die.

00:18:36.290 --> 00:18:38.090
I wanna go to heaven, but
I don't wanna go tonight.

00:18:38.090 --> 00:18:39.717
The main thing that I
thought about is the fact

00:18:39.717 --> 00:18:42.830
that I just don't feel as
prepared as Joe Diffie.

00:18:42.830 --> 00:18:44.610
Are they gonna do that to him?

00:18:44.610 --> 00:18:47.690
Are they gonna, is he
currently propped up beside,

00:18:47.690 --> 00:18:51.520
put some, put a stiff drink in my hand,

00:18:51.520 --> 00:18:53.030
fill my boots up with sand.

00:18:53.030 --> 00:18:55.240
Sadly, and I'm not joking,

00:18:55.240 --> 00:18:56.440
I know it seems that we're making light

00:18:56.440 --> 00:18:58.400
of Joe Diffie's death, we're not.

00:18:58.400 --> 00:19:01.140
I think the reason that
it's not gonna happen

00:19:01.140 --> 00:19:03.720
is because of this damn virus.

00:19:03.720 --> 00:19:06.530
It's like if he had died in
another way during normal times,

00:19:06.530 --> 00:19:09.360
they might actually be
considering that, but there's no,

00:19:09.360 --> 00:19:11.290
you can't have a funeral.
You can't.

00:19:11.290 --> 00:19:13.200
You can't have a funeral.

00:19:13.200 --> 00:19:14.033
I mean--

00:19:14.033 --> 00:19:14.928
You can have an online funeral.

00:19:14.928 --> 00:19:15.761
When you talk about famous people

00:19:15.761 --> 00:19:17.180
that you're fond of dying,

00:19:17.180 --> 00:19:21.960
I mean they're heart-wrenching,
tragic stories.

00:19:21.960 --> 00:19:25.830
I just can't bring myself
to read about 'em anymore,

00:19:25.830 --> 00:19:29.440
of people's loved ones passing away.

00:19:29.440 --> 00:19:31.910
And it's, you know, let's not get into it,

00:19:31.910 --> 00:19:36.910
but it's scary, and it's
taking too long to sink in,

00:19:37.100 --> 00:19:38.310
and it's taking,

00:19:38.310 --> 00:19:42.040
again, two weeks ago, it was said

00:19:42.040 --> 00:19:45.349
by the people who were
trying to get it to sink in,

00:19:45.349 --> 00:19:49.040
it's not gonna happen
until people start dying,

00:19:49.040 --> 00:19:50.950
until people you love start dying.

00:19:50.950 --> 00:19:53.380
Yeah, that's what it
takes for some people.

00:19:53.380 --> 00:19:54.900
We have this disconnect.

00:19:54.900 --> 00:19:55.733
We have this disconnect.

00:19:55.733 --> 00:19:58.670
You know, I operate under
this disconnect a lot.

00:19:58.670 --> 00:20:01.850
It's like you watch the news
and you hear about bad stories

00:20:01.850 --> 00:20:03.410
and it's like that stuff
happens to other people.

00:20:03.410 --> 00:20:04.280
It doesn't happen to me.

00:20:04.280 --> 00:20:06.379
It doesn't happen to people.

00:20:06.379 --> 00:20:08.220
It must be a defense mechanism,

00:20:08.220 --> 00:20:11.690
because I'd like to think
I'm not the only one

00:20:11.690 --> 00:20:14.170
who has that tendency.

00:20:14.170 --> 00:20:15.883
We talked about it some last week but--

00:20:15.883 --> 00:20:17.437
I think there's a number of reasons

00:20:17.437 --> 00:20:20.770
and we can--
There's a number of reasons.

00:20:20.770 --> 00:20:22.190
But I do think there are some positives,

00:20:22.190 --> 00:20:23.070
we'll get into that.

00:20:23.070 --> 00:20:24.130
We will get positive.

00:20:24.130 --> 00:20:26.110
But first, we're gonna
get positive right now

00:20:26.110 --> 00:20:27.750
'cause we're gonna let you know

00:20:27.750 --> 00:20:30.910
that we are continuing to sell the 3D--

00:20:30.910 --> 00:20:32.710
BYMB shirt.

00:20:32.710 --> 00:20:34.090
Be Your Mythical Best shirt,

00:20:34.090 --> 00:20:37.433
with a portion of the proceeds
going to Feeding America.

00:20:38.520 --> 00:20:39.540
And Link, do you have the copy

00:20:39.540 --> 00:20:41.560
for what Feeding America is doing?

00:20:41.560 --> 00:20:43.970
I had it, but my computer's
having so many problems,

00:20:43.970 --> 00:20:46.790
I had to shut the program
that had that information

00:20:46.790 --> 00:20:49.450
about what Feeding America
is specifically doing

00:20:49.450 --> 00:20:51.080
during the outbreak.

00:20:51.080 --> 00:20:54.250
Feeding America is a
nationwide network of 200,

00:20:54.250 --> 00:20:55.673
I can't even read numbers.
Oh, god.

00:20:55.673 --> 00:20:58.510
Wow.
I said 200 food banks

00:20:58.510 --> 00:21:01.010
working to ensure our most
vulnerable populations

00:21:01.010 --> 00:21:03.063
continue to have access to food.

00:21:05.301 --> 00:21:06.540
And their greatest need right now

00:21:06.540 --> 00:21:09.730
is donations and support
of local food banks.

00:21:09.730 --> 00:21:14.730
So yeah, the t-shirt
benefits Feeding America.

00:21:15.660 --> 00:21:18.150
You can also donate yourself, so.

00:21:18.150 --> 00:21:19.760
Mythical.com.

00:21:19.760 --> 00:21:23.730
So how do we wanna get
into some positivity?

00:21:23.730 --> 00:21:28.430
I wanna talk a little
bit about just this idea

00:21:28.430 --> 00:21:31.390
of conservation and how
that applies personally

00:21:31.390 --> 00:21:32.800
and what that might mean for the world--

00:21:32.800 --> 00:21:35.360
Oh, you're talking about leftovers?

00:21:35.360 --> 00:21:37.530
Yeah, well, I'm not going back

00:21:37.530 --> 00:21:41.640
to that specifically, but I
am saying that it has made,

00:21:41.640 --> 00:21:46.320
the scarcity or at least
the perceived scarcity

00:21:46.320 --> 00:21:50.600
of some of these goods,
toilet paper for who knows why

00:21:50.600 --> 00:21:53.870
being one of the chief ones that there's

00:21:53.870 --> 00:21:56.260
a perceived scarcity of toilet paper,

00:21:56.260 --> 00:21:57.790
that's not actually true,
there's just a few people

00:21:57.790 --> 00:21:59.400
who have it all.

00:21:59.400 --> 00:22:02.260
And I don't think they've got
especially dirty buttholes,

00:22:02.260 --> 00:22:04.783
I just think they got panicked.

00:22:06.740 --> 00:22:10.180
This perceived scarcity
has changed the way

00:22:10.180 --> 00:22:15.180
that I approach my meals,
but also the amount

00:22:15.310 --> 00:22:20.310
of toilet paper I do use, paper towels,

00:22:20.630 --> 00:22:23.080
like the McLaughlin family, we can go

00:22:23.080 --> 00:22:27.250
through a sapling's worth of paper towels

00:22:27.250 --> 00:22:31.100
in a meal sometimes, just a disregard

00:22:31.100 --> 00:22:33.719
for how much we are consuming.

00:22:33.719 --> 00:22:34.552
And this is a problem in America,

00:22:34.552 --> 00:22:36.330
this is a problem in the Western world,

00:22:36.330 --> 00:22:40.480
but the thing is is that as these

00:22:40.480 --> 00:22:43.830
developing nations become industrialized

00:22:43.830 --> 00:22:48.830
and they kind of move into
this consumer-based culture,

00:22:48.960 --> 00:22:50.980
they're all consuming
at the same rate, right.

00:22:50.980 --> 00:22:53.160
We know that's a problem.

00:22:53.160 --> 00:22:55.150
The Earth has been sort
of shouting for a while,

00:22:55.150 --> 00:22:58.207
like, "This is a problem,
there's not enough.

00:22:58.207 --> 00:23:00.440
"if you're all gonna act this way."

00:23:00.440 --> 00:23:04.000
But it makes me think, as
I was trying to evaluate

00:23:04.000 --> 00:23:05.840
what's this doing to me personally,

00:23:05.840 --> 00:23:08.240
I was thinking about Jessie's grandmother

00:23:08.240 --> 00:23:11.323
who died last year, I think she was 92.

00:23:12.830 --> 00:23:15.570
So she was basically born
right around the time

00:23:15.570 --> 00:23:20.210
of the Depression and she had

00:23:20.210 --> 00:23:22.840
this tendency to be incredibly,

00:23:22.840 --> 00:23:25.750
like she would conserve in
ways that we would laugh at.

00:23:25.750 --> 00:23:30.750
Like she would put aluminum
foil over some food,

00:23:31.070 --> 00:23:33.210
and then she would serve
it and then she would take

00:23:33.210 --> 00:23:36.200
the aluminum foil off,
and she would wash it,

00:23:36.200 --> 00:23:39.250
and she would fold it back up
and put it back in the drawer.

00:23:39.250 --> 00:23:42.540
I mean this woman used aluminum
foil over and over again.

00:23:42.540 --> 00:23:45.130
In fact, one time, one Christmas

00:23:45.130 --> 00:23:50.130
her sister, Aunt Betty,
got her aluminum foil,

00:23:50.300 --> 00:23:55.300
or tin foil as we called it
in the South, for Christmas.

00:23:55.630 --> 00:23:57.723
It's like that was your Christmas present.

00:23:58.680 --> 00:24:00.093
And those things that seem--

00:24:00.093 --> 00:24:02.250
So that she could throw
it away more readily

00:24:02.250 --> 00:24:04.470
or just because?
Just 'cause she knows

00:24:04.470 --> 00:24:06.580
how much she values it,
it's just like toilet paper

00:24:06.580 --> 00:24:07.963
has become currency.

00:24:09.777 --> 00:24:11.850
I haven't bought anything
with toilet paper.

00:24:11.850 --> 00:24:13.470
You could, try it.

00:24:13.470 --> 00:24:14.730
I could?
If you draw

00:24:14.730 --> 00:24:17.463
a picture of George Washington
on it, it'll definitely work.

00:24:18.530 --> 00:24:21.650
But yeah, just this idea that it's like,

00:24:21.650 --> 00:24:24.240
hmm, there is a limited amount of stuff.

00:24:24.240 --> 00:24:29.240
But not just that, but I think
it draws into focus for me

00:24:30.930 --> 00:24:35.930
this idea that the steady stream of goods

00:24:37.010 --> 00:24:38.750
is always going to be there.

00:24:38.750 --> 00:24:40.950
Like we took it for granted,

00:24:40.950 --> 00:24:43.740
that everything that we need
is always going to be there,

00:24:43.740 --> 00:24:46.110
you don't have to stock
up, you don't have to have

00:24:46.110 --> 00:24:49.040
extra of anything, you don't have to be

00:24:49.040 --> 00:24:50.360
prepared for anything.

00:24:50.360 --> 00:24:53.400
Now you know me and being a prepper light,

00:24:53.400 --> 00:24:56.880
I do have quite a bit of stuff ready.

00:24:56.880 --> 00:24:58.630
But it's not lifestyle stuff.

00:24:58.630 --> 00:25:00.420
Yeah, but it's like
emergency, I haven't gone

00:25:00.420 --> 00:25:02.447
into any of it yet 'cause it's
just liked freeze-dried food.

00:25:02.447 --> 00:25:04.840
But do you think, so you're
saying that you think

00:25:04.840 --> 00:25:07.810
this will affect your
lifestyle moving forward?

00:25:07.810 --> 00:25:10.943
I think it will and I think it will,

00:25:11.950 --> 00:25:15.813
my hope, is that it will
change the way our kids.

00:25:18.470 --> 00:25:21.090
The only people who really
have a sense of this

00:25:21.090 --> 00:25:24.520
are probably boomers,
'cause they were at least

00:25:24.520 --> 00:25:26.400
they can kind of remember
a time where things

00:25:26.400 --> 00:25:30.170
were a little bit less
available, but they've all

00:25:30.170 --> 00:25:32.700
bought into the same lie
that we've all bought into

00:25:32.700 --> 00:25:35.500
that we can get whatever
we want whenever we want it

00:25:35.500 --> 00:25:37.510
and damn the consequences.

00:25:37.510 --> 00:25:40.360
It doesn't matter what
that means about people.

00:25:40.360 --> 00:25:43.523
It doesn't matter what
it means about the Earth.

00:25:44.770 --> 00:25:47.410
But I think we're beginning to see that,

00:25:47.410 --> 00:25:50.073
no, there's consequences
to that hyperconsumerism.

00:25:52.380 --> 00:25:55.550
Not just this idea of
conservation as a, well,

00:25:55.550 --> 00:25:57.200
I don't know if it's gonna be there,

00:25:57.200 --> 00:25:59.563
but just beginning to examine,

00:26:01.110 --> 00:26:04.990
hold on, it's not just
that the supply chains

00:26:04.990 --> 00:26:07.380
are susceptible to things like a virus.

00:26:07.380 --> 00:26:09.994
It's not that the fact that
we've got a global economy

00:26:09.994 --> 00:26:13.290
and we're getting our
stuff from other places

00:26:13.290 --> 00:26:15.370
that then could be affected,
and we're all interdependent,

00:26:15.370 --> 00:26:18.593
and we're susceptible to
certain things, that's all true.

00:26:19.850 --> 00:26:23.640
But it just makes, for me
there's been a few things

00:26:23.640 --> 00:26:26.290
throughout this that have
just kinda begun to seem

00:26:26.290 --> 00:26:29.910
a little gross and one
of them is this idea

00:26:29.910 --> 00:26:34.180
that we can have whatever
we want, as much of it

00:26:34.180 --> 00:26:35.700
as we want whenever we want.

00:26:35.700 --> 00:26:36.753
It just seems like an irresponsible idea.

00:26:36.753 --> 00:26:41.250
But if it doesn't get practical,
it's not gonna take hold.

00:26:41.250 --> 00:26:43.710
I think it has to come down to, okay,

00:26:43.710 --> 00:26:45.203
I'm out of paper towels.

00:26:46.320 --> 00:26:49.160
I cannot get more paper towels.

00:26:49.160 --> 00:26:52.570
So I'm gonna start using hand towels

00:26:52.570 --> 00:26:55.060
and then I'm gonna put 'em in the laundry.

00:26:55.060 --> 00:26:58.110
We have a few rolls of paper towels left

00:26:58.110 --> 00:27:01.260
and I'm thinking about
just taking 'em out.

00:27:01.260 --> 00:27:03.193
'Cause we have hand towels.

00:27:04.330 --> 00:27:09.330
And once you discover a hand
towel works just as good

00:27:09.520 --> 00:27:12.040
as a paper towel, it's just,

00:27:12.040 --> 00:27:14.230
especially if you're a
kid and you're not the one

00:27:14.230 --> 00:27:19.230
laundering the hand towels,
there's no impact to you.

00:27:19.510 --> 00:27:21.910
Except now that I've wiped up this thing,

00:27:21.910 --> 00:27:23.460
or I've wiped my hands, or whatever,

00:27:23.460 --> 00:27:25.560
I don't know where to put it.

00:27:25.560 --> 00:27:28.420
Well, you need to hang
it up and let it dry

00:27:28.420 --> 00:27:29.600
and you can use it again.

00:27:29.600 --> 00:27:31.890
You don't have to just throw it away.

00:27:31.890 --> 00:27:35.030
So it's really just a habit.

00:27:35.030 --> 00:27:38.090
And it's once you exchange
to doing the hand towels

00:27:38.090 --> 00:27:41.890
and you get used to it for
a few weeks or a few months,

00:27:41.890 --> 00:27:44.597
I think at that point then you can say,

00:27:44.597 --> 00:27:46.377
"You know what, let's not go back."

00:27:47.850 --> 00:27:50.120
I'm using much less toilet paper.

00:27:50.120 --> 00:27:53.340
I'm using my bidet to a whole new level.

00:27:53.340 --> 00:27:55.140
Oh, the bidet is the smartest thing

00:27:55.140 --> 00:27:56.350
I've ever bought in my life.

00:27:56.350 --> 00:27:59.410
But I realized how much
toilet paper I was still using

00:27:59.410 --> 00:28:00.840
in combination with my bidet.

00:28:00.840 --> 00:28:04.120
And I'm like, you know what,
I shouldn't have to use

00:28:06.110 --> 00:28:10.410
two squares, and that's just
to dab off, just to dry off.

00:28:10.410 --> 00:28:12.880
There is an air dryer,
but it takes forever.

00:28:12.880 --> 00:28:16.190
Takes too long, and it
doesn't really work that well.

00:28:16.190 --> 00:28:20.363
It also kind of sends toilet
stank throughout your room.

00:28:21.670 --> 00:28:23.850
I think when you,

00:28:23.850 --> 00:28:28.100
it can't be an ideological change of mind,

00:28:28.100 --> 00:28:30.690
it has to be a habitual change of mind,

00:28:30.690 --> 00:28:35.330
and I think as the weeks
go on, that's what will

00:28:35.330 --> 00:28:36.393
make the difference.

00:28:39.688 --> 00:28:41.590
But then I think there's, and
I think that's a positive.

00:28:41.590 --> 00:28:42.423
I think another positive when I'm talking

00:28:42.423 --> 00:28:44.810
about the groceries
coming in and I'm talking

00:28:44.810 --> 00:28:46.820
about how stressful
that is and everything,

00:28:46.820 --> 00:28:49.280
but I'm also thinking, you know what,

00:28:49.280 --> 00:28:52.050
it took me 30 minutes
to wipe everything down,

00:28:52.050 --> 00:28:54.370
but in the long run, when this is over,

00:28:54.370 --> 00:28:56.050
I'm not gonna be wiping anything down,

00:28:56.050 --> 00:28:58.400
and by the way, how
long would it have taken

00:28:58.400 --> 00:29:00.390
to shop for all these groceries

00:29:01.430 --> 00:29:03.350
that I just spent all
this time wiping down

00:29:03.350 --> 00:29:04.870
if they weren't delivered to me?

00:29:04.870 --> 00:29:06.260
I saved all of that time.

00:29:06.260 --> 00:29:09.160
Man, grocery delivery's pretty nice.

00:29:09.160 --> 00:29:12.750
Even if I don't get exactly
what I want, they swap it out

00:29:12.750 --> 00:29:15.380
with something else, and there's
a little bit of a surprise.

00:29:15.380 --> 00:29:17.000
You don't know exactly
what you're gonna get.

00:29:17.000 --> 00:29:18.710
Ooh, look at what I got today.

00:29:18.710 --> 00:29:22.990
It feels like I'm going back
to a milkman experience, man.

00:29:22.990 --> 00:29:24.480
I always wondered what that would be like.

00:29:24.480 --> 00:29:25.840
Ooh, look, the milkman's here, I wonder

00:29:25.840 --> 00:29:27.170
what kinda milk I got.

00:29:27.170 --> 00:29:28.930
Is it strawberry, is it chocolate?

00:29:28.930 --> 00:29:30.960
Did they do that?
I don't think so.

00:29:30.960 --> 00:29:32.360
I think it was mostly just milk.

00:29:32.360 --> 00:29:34.853
Unless your milkman was a Nesquik rabbit.

00:29:35.740 --> 00:29:37.653
Mine wasn't, 'cause I never had one.

00:29:39.640 --> 00:29:42.970
There's some people who I know they do

00:29:44.130 --> 00:29:47.560
the grocery delivery, but
the vast, vast, vast majority

00:29:47.560 --> 00:29:48.960
of people don't do that.

00:29:48.960 --> 00:29:51.540
You go and you shop for
your groceries, because,

00:29:51.540 --> 00:29:53.740
well you know, I'll remember
something else that I need,

00:29:53.740 --> 00:29:55.423
or this will strike my fancy.

00:29:56.300 --> 00:29:59.590
But we're actually learning,
even with so many things

00:29:59.590 --> 00:30:02.770
being swapped out that we
got a crap ton of groceries

00:30:02.770 --> 00:30:05.540
come yesterday and it's
gonna last us a few weeks,

00:30:05.540 --> 00:30:07.090
'cause we're trying to minimize the amount

00:30:07.090 --> 00:30:12.090
of touchpoints that we
have with new deliveries.

00:30:12.220 --> 00:30:14.583
I think that's something
that's gonna keep going.

00:30:16.420 --> 00:30:20.430
The way that shopping has moved to Amazon

00:30:20.430 --> 00:30:23.973
over the past five years,

00:30:25.400 --> 00:30:28.140
whenever I want something the
first thing I think is Amazon,

00:30:28.140 --> 00:30:29.393
except for groceries.

00:30:30.370 --> 00:30:32.330
And I think this is
putting me over the edge

00:30:32.330 --> 00:30:35.156
to have groceries delivered.

00:30:35.156 --> 00:30:37.156
So I think that's another silver lining.

00:30:40.080 --> 00:30:42.043
I find that somewhat problematic.

00:30:45.530 --> 00:30:47.630
The whole Amazon thing,

00:30:47.630 --> 00:30:52.140
you know, Amazon has its own issues

00:30:52.140 --> 00:30:54.780
that it's dealing with.

00:30:54.780 --> 00:30:59.240
I think that any major company like that

00:30:59.240 --> 00:31:03.010
that is trying to serve that many people

00:31:03.010 --> 00:31:05.750
and do it for profit.

00:31:05.750 --> 00:31:07.090
I'm not saying order from Amazon,

00:31:07.090 --> 00:31:09.670
I'm saying order from
your local grocery store.

00:31:09.670 --> 00:31:11.400
I'm saying that habit,
I think, will continue.

00:31:11.400 --> 00:31:14.680
But I'm saying you have to evaluate

00:31:15.880 --> 00:31:18.170
that may make your life
easier and it may get you

00:31:18.170 --> 00:31:21.420
off of the road, but it is also putting

00:31:21.420 --> 00:31:23.640
a delivery person on the road meaning that

00:31:23.640 --> 00:31:25.920
from a carbon standpoint,

00:31:25.920 --> 00:31:28.290
I don't know, I've never looked into this,

00:31:28.290 --> 00:31:30.850
I don't know which one is mo-

00:31:30.850 --> 00:31:33.340
I think one of the things
is like I have a tendency,

00:31:33.340 --> 00:31:35.610
for instance, when I
order stuff from Amazon,

00:31:35.610 --> 00:31:38.390
as I think of it, I'll just order it

00:31:38.390 --> 00:31:41.543
and it's one trip, it's one box,

00:31:42.610 --> 00:31:44.950
whereas there's a more
responsible way to do that

00:31:44.950 --> 00:31:46.700
to put things into a larger order.

00:31:46.700 --> 00:31:48.870
Just that's a small thing that then, okay,

00:31:48.870 --> 00:31:52.770
I can make this one trip
from one delivery person

00:31:52.770 --> 00:31:54.683
as opposed to three or four.

00:31:57.150 --> 00:31:58.590
But I do think that some of the--

00:31:58.590 --> 00:32:01.040
But what I'm asking is what,

00:32:01.040 --> 00:32:03.340
we're really talking
about what in the future,

00:32:04.390 --> 00:32:06.060
how are our future
lives gonna be different

00:32:06.060 --> 00:32:07.970
because of this experience?

00:32:07.970 --> 00:32:09.650
And I'm just saying I'm gonna be ordering

00:32:09.650 --> 00:32:12.500
a bunch of things that
I didn't order before.

00:32:12.500 --> 00:32:16.103
I think groceries is
the chief amongst those.

00:32:17.000 --> 00:32:18.860
Well, let's talk about
what you were talking

00:32:18.860 --> 00:32:22.360
about earlier which is,
we can get back more

00:32:22.360 --> 00:32:25.713
into the societal, financial,

00:32:26.720 --> 00:32:29.773
sciencey stuff in a
second, but the greeting,

00:32:30.687 --> 00:32:33.370
the idea of greetings.

00:32:33.370 --> 00:32:35.530
Now obviously at some point,

00:32:35.530 --> 00:32:38.640
I don't think I'll ever
go back to a handshake.

00:32:38.640 --> 00:32:39.473
Just to put it simply.

00:32:39.473 --> 00:32:40.480
Well, and 'cause there is

00:32:40.480 --> 00:32:42.210
an acceptable alternative for it.

00:32:42.210 --> 00:32:43.990
But before we get to
the handshakes, though,

00:32:43.990 --> 00:32:47.873
just the idea of being in closed
spaces with people, right.

00:32:49.710 --> 00:32:52.550
I mean one of the things
that I've been noticing

00:32:52.550 --> 00:32:57.270
as I watch television and
I see groups of people,

00:32:57.270 --> 00:32:59.300
like crowded places, I can't help

00:32:59.300 --> 00:33:01.590
but think about the virus.

00:33:01.590 --> 00:33:04.460
Now that's gonna go away,
like we're not gonna be

00:33:04.460 --> 00:33:07.630
a species that no longer gathers together

00:33:07.630 --> 00:33:09.040
in public spaces.

00:33:09.040 --> 00:33:14.040
But I mean this particular,
depending on who you talk to,

00:33:14.390 --> 00:33:16.200
and there's a range of predictions,

00:33:16.200 --> 00:33:20.420
but this is probably going to be something

00:33:20.420 --> 00:33:22.450
that subsides a little bit over the summer

00:33:22.450 --> 00:33:24.980
and then comes back again in the fall.

00:33:24.980 --> 00:33:28.300
It looks like it's gonna
have some seasonality to it.

00:33:28.300 --> 00:33:33.300
We're not gonna have a
vaccine by the fall, I doubt.

00:33:33.460 --> 00:33:35.430
I mean that would be an
unprecedented timeline,

00:33:35.430 --> 00:33:36.490
maybe that'll happen.

00:33:36.490 --> 00:33:40.350
But it doesn't look it will
and so we may actually be

00:33:40.350 --> 00:33:44.060
dealing with it even a year after that.

00:33:44.060 --> 00:33:47.130
So this particular virus may be something

00:33:47.130 --> 00:33:52.130
that affects public gatherings
for 18 to 24 months.

00:33:52.670 --> 00:33:56.830
But I think eventually
when we get past this,

00:33:56.830 --> 00:33:58.130
we're gonna be thinking
differently about it,

00:33:58.130 --> 00:34:01.434
but the thing is, and
again, comparisons between--

00:34:01.434 --> 00:34:03.460
Here's how I'm gonna be
thinking differently about it.

00:34:03.460 --> 00:34:05.250
I'm not gonna shake hands.

00:34:05.250 --> 00:34:07.840
I'm not gonna be touching railings.

00:34:07.840 --> 00:34:10.653
Like all the stuff that everybody touches,

00:34:13.560 --> 00:34:15.950
I'm gonna have an instinct
to not touch those things

00:34:15.950 --> 00:34:20.240
because now I have an
appreciation for how easily things

00:34:20.240 --> 00:34:22.820
can be on, how germs can be on there.

00:34:22.820 --> 00:34:25.270
Like I'm gonna be more
of a germaphobe, man.

00:34:25.270 --> 00:34:28.440
I think I'm just gonna
have my hand in my sleeve

00:34:28.440 --> 00:34:30.640
and I'm gonna be opening doorknobs.

00:34:30.640 --> 00:34:35.220
I already whenever I would push on a door,

00:34:35.220 --> 00:34:38.260
I would think about the
place where the least

00:34:38.260 --> 00:34:40.910
number of people push,
especially children.

00:34:40.910 --> 00:34:43.940
So I'll reach really high
and then push on a door.

00:34:43.940 --> 00:34:44.821
Oh yeah, I do that all the time.

00:34:44.821 --> 00:34:45.980
I've been doing that for years.

00:34:45.980 --> 00:34:49.590
But I couldn't even tell you
exactly why I was doing it.

00:34:49.590 --> 00:34:52.460
I was like, yeah, 'cause a
lot of people touch there

00:34:52.460 --> 00:34:53.910
and that's gross.

00:34:53.910 --> 00:34:57.870
But I hadn't really made the connection

00:34:57.870 --> 00:35:02.500
that now is firmly in place
that's there is something there

00:35:02.500 --> 00:35:04.990
that then will be on me that
then will go in my mouth.

00:35:04.990 --> 00:35:06.810
I'm Howie Mandel-ing this thing, man.

00:35:06.810 --> 00:35:07.729
Well but the question is is that--

00:35:07.729 --> 00:35:10.410
I don't think I'm ever
gonna go all the way back.

00:35:10.410 --> 00:35:15.410
But my question is if
everyone began to beh-,

00:35:15.460 --> 00:35:17.820
I'm split on this, because
I feel like if everyone

00:35:17.820 --> 00:35:22.160
behaves in this hypervigilant
way during normal times,

00:35:22.160 --> 00:35:24.930
not now, but during normal times,

00:35:24.930 --> 00:35:27.070
everybody's using hand
sanitizer, everybody's avoiding

00:35:27.070 --> 00:35:29.340
each other, nobody's talking,
I mean nobody's touching,

00:35:29.340 --> 00:35:31.200
nobody's shaking hands,
nobody's touching doorknobs

00:35:31.200 --> 00:35:32.870
and stuff like that, everybody's putting

00:35:32.870 --> 00:35:34.470
hand sanitizer on every surface.

00:35:36.290 --> 00:35:40.500
I need to talk to an expert
on this, but my thought is

00:35:42.140 --> 00:35:45.960
that that may make us
as a species long term

00:35:45.960 --> 00:35:47.350
more susceptible to things.

00:35:47.350 --> 00:35:51.160
I mean one of the reasons that when,

00:35:51.160 --> 00:35:54.740
if you read "Guns, Germs, and
Steel," one of the reasons

00:35:54.740 --> 00:35:59.740
that when the Western Europeans
when they came to America,

00:36:01.830 --> 00:36:04.980
very few of them died and many
of the Native Americans died

00:36:04.980 --> 00:36:06.510
was because--
'Cause the Native Americans

00:36:06.510 --> 00:36:08.500
did not have doorknobs.

00:36:08.500 --> 00:36:12.100
Yeah, they had been living
in these open spaces

00:36:12.100 --> 00:36:13.400
and they weren't crowded together,

00:36:13.400 --> 00:36:15.450
but all the Western
Europeans had been in cities

00:36:15.450 --> 00:36:16.590
and gathered together so they had

00:36:16.590 --> 00:36:18.190
all this resistance to viruses.

00:36:18.190 --> 00:36:22.270
We're gonna keep, these
viruses are gonna keep coming.

00:36:22.270 --> 00:36:24.620
You're gonna have a lot
more deaths from falling

00:36:24.620 --> 00:36:27.100
because people aren't
gonna be using handrails,

00:36:27.100 --> 00:36:31.320
people aren't gonna be
steadying themselves.

00:36:31.320 --> 00:36:34.780
Like I'm gonna, this is
how I'm gonna go now,

00:36:34.780 --> 00:36:38.480
because of this experience,
I'm gonna fall down something

00:36:38.480 --> 00:36:39.490
No, but what I'm telling you
because I didn't want

00:36:39.490 --> 00:36:40.610
to touch it.
Is that if everyone

00:36:40.610 --> 00:36:43.270
does what you're doing,
it may actually make us

00:36:43.270 --> 00:36:45.240
more susceptible to the next round,

00:36:45.240 --> 00:36:47.490
because there will be another round.

00:36:47.490 --> 00:36:50.770
I will have fallen down
stairs before that happens,

00:36:50.770 --> 00:36:52.110
but yeah, as a society.

00:36:52.110 --> 00:36:56.140
But it makes me think
differently about just flu season

00:36:56.140 --> 00:36:58.570
in general, I don't
think about flu season.

00:36:58.570 --> 00:37:01.250
And I know that comparisons
between COVID and flu

00:37:01.250 --> 00:37:03.363
are unhelpful, very unhelpful.

00:37:04.570 --> 00:37:07.680
But the fact is is that the seasonal flu,

00:37:07.680 --> 00:37:10.653
if it's a bad one, kills
thousands of people,

00:37:11.634 --> 00:37:12.540
well it kills people every year,

00:37:12.540 --> 00:37:14.820
sometimes tens of thousands of people,

00:37:14.820 --> 00:37:18.350
and I don't get the flu shot.

00:37:18.350 --> 00:37:21.336
I mean, I very rarely get the flu shot.

00:37:21.336 --> 00:37:23.640
And the reason I don't
get it is because I feel

00:37:23.640 --> 00:37:27.177
personally invincible, so I think like,

00:37:27.177 --> 00:37:30.686
"Oh, I might get the flu,
but it's not gonna kill me,

00:37:30.686 --> 00:37:32.210
"I have to make an appointment."

00:37:32.210 --> 00:37:34.963
But now I'm thinking about my own,

00:37:36.610 --> 00:37:38.200
I'm not just thinking
about my own situation,

00:37:38.200 --> 00:37:43.132
I'm thinking about how I
contribute to everyone's situation.

00:37:43.132 --> 00:37:44.800
I never thought about the flu shot

00:37:44.800 --> 00:37:45.843
Me getting the flu shot
In light of society service.

00:37:45.843 --> 00:37:48.830
Is not about me, it's about society.

00:37:48.830 --> 00:37:51.340
Getting the flu shot is
about the people who I might

00:37:51.340 --> 00:37:53.660
come in contact with that are susceptible

00:37:53.660 --> 00:37:55.207
and not being the a-hole, who's like,

00:37:55.207 --> 00:37:58.257
"Oh, I might get the flu
and then I might interact

00:37:58.257 --> 00:38:00.490
"with someone who's
susceptible and they die."

00:38:00.490 --> 00:38:03.900
It's like, oh, that's a big change for me.

00:38:03.900 --> 00:38:07.030
But I do think it's gonna also,

00:38:07.030 --> 00:38:08.880
I think the greeting thing is a big thing.

00:38:08.880 --> 00:38:11.600
Because there's currently an alternative.

00:38:11.600 --> 00:38:14.990
And there's no way, there's zero chance

00:38:14.990 --> 00:38:17.260
that shaking of hands and getting together

00:38:17.260 --> 00:38:19.667
and getting really close to each other

00:38:19.667 --> 00:38:23.390
in tight environments is gonna
be a regular thing in 2020,

00:38:23.390 --> 00:38:25.663
like that's not coming back in 2020.

00:38:27.520 --> 00:38:29.144
I'm not saying there
won't be some gatherings,

00:38:29.144 --> 00:38:31.490
but it's not coming back soon.

00:38:31.490 --> 00:38:35.650
I mean, when concerts and
going to the movies and parties

00:38:35.650 --> 00:38:37.810
start happening again, boy, I think

00:38:37.810 --> 00:38:40.750
there'll be a little bit of
time where we appreciate it.

00:38:40.750 --> 00:38:43.357
And then there'll be a time
when we're old when we'll say,

00:38:43.357 --> 00:38:46.506
"Oh, you know, you can go to
parties any time you want,

00:38:46.506 --> 00:38:49.177
"but there was a time
when we couldn't do that.

