And it will happen more often, actually,

01:03:21.580 --> 01:03:25.789
the more we continue to grow,
and there's more people,

01:03:25.789 --> 01:03:26.622
and that we're more crowded.

01:03:26.622 --> 01:03:28.800
Travel and connections, I
mean it's a lot different

01:03:28.800 --> 01:03:30.990
than it was 100 years ago.

01:03:30.990 --> 01:03:34.870
But I think that the good
news, something that we can

01:03:34.870 --> 01:03:36.700
be hopeful about of what you said

01:03:36.700 --> 01:03:39.600
is that, first of all,
there are things happening

01:03:39.600 --> 01:03:42.810
as a result of this virus
that directly impact

01:03:42.810 --> 01:03:43.800
carbon emissions, right.

01:03:43.800 --> 01:03:46.928
And I'm not just talking about
factories being shut down,

01:03:46.928 --> 01:03:51.080
or all the sudden there's
no tourism in Venice, Italy

01:03:51.080 --> 01:03:54.000
so the canals are clear for the
first time in recent memory.

01:03:54.000 --> 01:03:55.890
That's not a sustainable
thing, we don't want there

01:03:55.890 --> 01:03:57.960
to be no tourists, we
want people to travel,

01:03:57.960 --> 01:03:59.700
we want people to make things, we do want

01:03:59.700 --> 01:04:01.790
the economy to come back.

01:04:01.790 --> 01:04:06.790
But something as simple
as thinking differently

01:04:06.990 --> 01:04:08.800
about consumerism and consumption

01:04:10.100 --> 01:04:14.150
that is going to have an
impact on just the amount

01:04:14.150 --> 01:04:16.227
of goods that are produced

01:04:16.227 --> 01:04:18.770
and what we think we actually need.

01:04:18.770 --> 01:04:20.360
And I think that that is gonna have

01:04:20.360 --> 01:04:22.550
a positive impact on climate.

01:04:22.550 --> 01:04:24.640
These supply chains being so global,

01:04:24.640 --> 01:04:28.290
which I think it's great that
we're connected as humans

01:04:28.290 --> 01:04:31.600
and the imaginary borders
that we place around ourselves

01:04:31.600 --> 01:04:33.830
we're beginning to see
that they are just that,

01:04:33.830 --> 01:04:35.810
they're imaginary, you
can't keep the virus out,

01:04:35.810 --> 01:04:37.730
the virus doesn't know
that there's a border

01:04:37.730 --> 01:04:40.290
around your country, doesn't see people

01:04:40.290 --> 01:04:42.370
the way that people see people.

01:04:42.370 --> 01:04:47.370
But we necessarily will
have to move manufacturing

01:04:47.650 --> 01:04:50.890
closer to the people who
need those manufactured goods

01:04:50.890 --> 01:04:53.240
and that is going to
have a drastic impact.

01:04:53.240 --> 01:04:56.580
'Cause basically when all this
stuff got outsourced globally

01:04:56.580 --> 01:04:58.740
and all the stuff is being
produced in mass quantities

01:04:58.740 --> 01:05:01.270
and then shipped all around the world,

01:05:01.270 --> 01:05:04.770
all of that has increased
carbon emissions drastically,

01:05:04.770 --> 01:05:07.800
but if we bring those supply
chains back closer to home,

01:05:07.800 --> 01:05:09.790
it'll be good for local
economies, it'll be good

01:05:09.790 --> 01:05:13.250
for the environment, so I think that part

01:05:13.250 --> 01:05:15.770
of the economic recovery because of this

01:05:15.770 --> 01:05:18.020
could actually have some of those

01:05:18.020 --> 01:05:20.773
green technologies and strategies,

01:05:21.760 --> 01:05:25.780
which ultimately will
in the long term make us

01:05:26.770 --> 01:05:29.070
healthier as an economy and be able

01:05:29.070 --> 01:05:32.963
to address the pressing
problems of climate change.

01:05:37.000 --> 01:05:37.833
I'm trying to be hopeful.

01:05:37.833 --> 01:05:38.666
There is hope, Link.

01:05:38.666 --> 01:05:39.860
I'm trying to be hopeful.

01:05:40.890 --> 01:05:42.233
